      "content": "Research indicates that media often employ more negative and dehumanizing vocabulary when covering protests involving marginalized groups compared to those organized by established institutions. As demonstrations escalate in intensity, framing frequently shifts to emphasize \"public order\" and \"risk,\" frequently using words like \"riot\" or \"unrest\" rather than \"protest\" or \"demonstration.\" This linguistic variation suggests a subconscious bias where the identity of the performers and the perceived disruption level significantly shape the perceived legitimacy of the event in newspaper and broadcast headlines.",
